WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.780
Too many deaths, or not enough of them?

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.430
And what happened to Peter's helmet?

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:09.500
"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3" may have
just touched down in theaters, and fans have

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.500
thoughts.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.580
Spoilers ahead for "Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 3."

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.100
James Gunn wanting to work with his wife,
Jennifer Holland, is apparently the internet's

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.020
equivalent of wearing socks with sandals to
the mall.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.010
The usual suspects of Film Twitter have painted
the narrative that Gunn always finds a way

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.970
to cast Holland in his productions, and they
see it as a form of Hollywood nepotism.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:33.210
Considering Holland appears as Administrator
Kwol in "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3,"

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.010
her cameo has stirred up the conversation
again.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:40.340
Truth be told, Kwol's role in the story is
minor, and it isn't like Gunn threw out an

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.230
established performer to make room for his
wife on screen.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.720
Some fans have come to Holland and Gunn's
defense, pointing out how the filmmaker has

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.410
often cast the same actors in his movies before
and there has never been any complaints about

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.410
it.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.410
One tweeted:

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.850
"It's no secret there's a subset of geek fandom
that are unashamedly misogynistic [...] but

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.820
the hate for Jennifer Holland being a James
Gunn mainstay is so clearly sexist when you

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.290
just start naming Michael Rooker, Sean Gunn,
Nathan Fillion, and so on…"

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.270
In the build-up to "Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 3," fans were excited by the prospect

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.229
of seeing Adam Warlock make his live-action
debut.

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:18.090
The character had been teased before in the
MCU, but he would now be portrayed in flesh

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.880
and blood by Will Poulter.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:25.229
But this version of the being first introduced
as Him in the pages of 1967's "Fantastic Four"

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:28.170
comics is much different than fans might have
imagined.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.070
One redditor said Poulter was "a weird choice"
to play the character, and added that the

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.950
makeup was, quote, "TNG side character tier."

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.820
"It became pretty clear they weren't going
for perfect when they cast me."

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:38.820
"Oh stop!

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:39.820
You're so cute."

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.300
"They were going in a different direction
for the role."

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.659
For some viewers, the role isn't as substantial
as it should have been, with Poulter's Warlock

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:48.030
featuring scarcely in the film.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.030
Another redditor said:

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.550
"Nothing against his performance but I feel
like you could cut him out of the movie and

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:56.430
nothing would be lost, his connection to the
plot was very flimsy."

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.869
Others are not impressed by how his personality
has been diluted here, with James Gunn making

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:04.119
him a comedic and somewhat childlike fish-out-of-water
character.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:07.960
One Redditor described him as "yet another
himbo idiot."

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.260
In "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2," the audience
is introduced to the Golden High Priestess

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.170
of the Sovereign, a race of beings created
by the High Evolutionary.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.890
Ayesha is a minor antagonist but she plays
a vital role in the creation of Adam Warlock,

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.840
which is teased in the post-credits scene
of the film.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.010
"I think I shall call him…Adam."

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.780
In "Guardians 3," Ayesha returns with her
"son," Adam, in tow as they do the High Evolutionary's

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:34.959
evil bidding.

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:39.519
Unfortunately, she is blown to smithereens
on Counter-Earth and presumably doesn't survive

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:41.120
the events of the movie.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.260
While Ayesha does have a few sporadic scenes
scattered throughout, there are a few fans

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.200
puzzled by her overall purpose in this storyline.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:53.150
As one Redditor suggested, there was an opportunity
to see both her and Warlock face off against

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.959
the High Evolutionary and bring closure to
that part of the story.

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:00.700
Instead, she becomes glorified cannon fodder.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.860
Since the first time fans met Rocket, it's
clear he went through some trauma in his past.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.970
He doesn't like to talk about it, and his
history has been largely shrouded in secrecy

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.200
— until now.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.209
"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3" shows him
as a tiny and cute-as-a-button baby raccoon

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:18.080
that becomes a part of High Evolutionary's
sick experiments.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.660
It's made even worse when the viewers meet
Rocket's three new adorable friends — Lylla

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.280
the otter, Teefs the walrus, and Floor the
rabbit — and see what has been done to them

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.280
as well.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.530
Sure, they are upbeat about life in general,
but there's no disputing they have been tortured.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:36.600
While James Gunn doesn't get graphic in the
depiction of animal abuse, it's the things

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.140
left unsaid that leave an unsettling feeling
in the pit of the stomach.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.260
This hasn't been lost on the fans, either,
as the animal lovers have expressed how difficult

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:47.620
it is to watch these scenes and not be impacted
by it.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.780
Of course, this is the intention of the filmmakers.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.650
Animal lovers on Reddit said they were brought
to tears, with one sharing:

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.409
"When Lylla was shot I looked over and saw
my wife crying."

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:02.020
The MCU loves to sprinkle its films and TV
shows with humorous moments and jokes as a

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.430
way to provide levity to the stories.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.189
The Guardians have always been the biggest
jokers of the pack, and their appearances

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:11.530
have contained the most amount of humor out
of all the MCU projects.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:16.160
While the trailers for "Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 3" showed off a darker, serious tone

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.760
than most fans might have expected, no one
should have been surprised that the film would

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.170
find a way to incorporate some laughs.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:26.900
Most of the comic relief comes from the interactions
between Drax the Destroyer and Mantis, but

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.169
there's also a recurring gag between Cosmo
the Spacedog and Kraglin.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:34.090
Some, however, have criticized the film for
having lame jokes.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:35.090
One moviegoer tweeted:

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:39.410
"A good chunk of the jokes were pretty unfunny
and it seemed like Gunn didn't know what to

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.280
do with a lot of the cast."

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.759
Another said they, quote, "cringed during
A LOT of the humor this time around."

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:49.530
Make no mistake about it — comic book fans
are among the most perceptive people in the

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.530
world.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:53.450
They miss nothing, and will let filmmakers
know if something is amiss.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.660
One of the hottest debates surrounding "Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 3" is about the lack of

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.070
Star-Lord's helmet.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.040
Unlike the past movies, Peter Quill doesn't
utilize it here, and many fans have wondered

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.820
why it is missing from the action.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.690
It is considered an important part of the
hero's attire, so it is in the same league

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:10.720
as Captain America and his mighty shield.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.259
A Reddit thread about the topic has been started
with one commentator suggesting that Star-Lord

00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:18.540
wouldn't have almost died in space had he
activated his helmet.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.850
Another user countered the argument by saying
that Quill and the others left in a hurry

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:26.009
to try and save Rocket, so he may have forgotten
his helmet back at the base.

00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:29.420
The "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 3" trailers
promised sadness.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.569
Fans feared the worst for a member of the
crew — with most presuming Rocket would

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:33.710
be the one to perish.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.970
And because this marks the end of the trilogy,
it was always likely that someone would get

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.160
killed as part of a narrative beat.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.940
In the end, all the Guardians are safe, and
no one heads off to Valhalla to meet up with

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:44.940
Jane Foster.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.430
"I see you're dead now."

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:48.430
"Ugh.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:49.430
Yeah."

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:51.250
But that doesn't mean the other characters
are quite as lucky.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:56.040
In one of the most heart-shattering scenes
of the film, Lylla, Teefs, and Floor don't

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.690
get to see the blue sky with Rocket as he
prepares to escape in a flashback sequence.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.840
They might have been fresh characters introduced
in this film, but these three wholesome creatures

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.120
leave a significant mark with their kindness.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.889
Many viewers have expressed how horrified
they were to see the events unfold.

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:12.150
According to one Redditor:

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:17.289
"I just knew Lila, Teeths and Floor were going
to die but their deaths really got me."

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:21.190
Forget about Peter Parker telling Tony Stark
that he doesn't feel so good — this is now

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:24.800
officially the saddest moment in a Marvel
movie.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.470
There are several moments in "Guardians of
the Galaxy Vol. 3" where James Gunn pulls

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:32.370
the rug out from under the viewers and makes
everyone believe that someone is a goner.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:36.949
A particularly harrowing scene occurs toward
the end of the film when Star-Lord doesn't

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:39.639
escape in time and floats through space.

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:43.190
His body begins to freeze and his face bloats
to look like a pufferfish.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:46.900
Adam Warlock becomes the hero as he flies
out to Peter Quill and saves him.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.900
The puffiness disappears from Quill's face
not too long afterward, and it seems like

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.949
it was only intended for a gag.

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:55.949
Even so, there are fans who don't understand
the point of this.

00:06:55.949 --> 00:06:59.831
One Redditor suggested that it made Quill
look more like Pedro Pascal, while another

00:06:59.831 --> 00:07:03.050
reminded everyone that this wouldn't have
happened had he worn his helmet.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:08.800
"We'll all fly away together, one last time."

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.470
The ending of "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol.
3" signifies the end of a chapter and the

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:13.840
start of a new one.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.100
The core team is dismantled, as the majority
of the Guardians go off in separate directions

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:19.819
to find what they need in their lives.

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:23.699
In their place, a new set of Guardians, led
by Rocket, steps out in the mid-credits scene

00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:27.470
— presumably as the new iteration of the
group for the MCU.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.849
While it was always clear that this would
be James Gunn and many cast members' last

00:07:30.849 --> 00:07:34.060
hoorah, this turn of events still upset a
lot of fans.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.060
One Twitter user said:

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.570
"Was sad that the group split up but I'm hopeful
to see what the future holds."

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:42.410
Farewells are never easy, but this wasn't
necessarily a "goodbye" — more of a "see

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:43.419
you later."

00:07:43.419 --> 00:07:46.819
Considering that all of these characters are
still alive in the MCU, there's always the

00:07:46.819 --> 00:07:49.050
chance that their paths could cross again.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:53.169
Each member of the Guardians of the Galaxy
suffers in the third film, but they all get

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:54.920
their happily ever after in the end.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:59.310
We see some strong character progressions
and developments as the core characters realize

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.871
there are aspects of their lives they need
to work on independently, but they are all

00:08:02.871 --> 00:08:07.409
able to sail off into the sunset and smile
at their friends while doing so.

00:08:07.409 --> 00:08:08.690
"Hahahaha.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:09.970
Hey!"

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:13.870
Some fans feel let down by this decision to
give everyone a peaceful ending.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:18.030
They expected bloodshed and waterfalls of
tears as the group got ripped apart and destroyed

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.080
on screen.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.550
The fans also aren't too pleased by the fakeouts:
We're led to believe that Drax, Star-Lord,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.659
and Rocket have been wiped out for good.

00:08:25.659 --> 00:08:26.780
One Redditor explained:

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:31.590
"I'm fine with no member dying even though
they hinted so strongly at it, but doing 3

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:37.110
fake out deaths is just overkill imo, especially
having one straight up flatline."

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:41.000
Some even negatively compared this to "Star
Wars: The Rise of Skywalker," which annoyed

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.750
fans by doing a similar thing.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.450
Be that as it may, this is the MCU — not
"Game of Thrones."

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:47.510
Unlike George R.R.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:51.510
Martin, Kevin Feige isn't casually killing
off main characters.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:55.810
In the post-credits scene of "The Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 3," text splashes on the

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:59.880
screen, stating: "The legendary Star-Lord
will return," while the mid-credits scene

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.399
showcases a new version of the team.

00:09:02.399 --> 00:09:05.740
What this indicates is that the Guardians'
story isn't quite over yet.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:10.110
Sure, the original incarnation of the squad
might not be teaming up anytime soon, but

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.920
there are more cosmic tales left in the MCU
tank.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:16.750
While this will be good news to many fans,
there are others who are annoyed by this.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:21.730
One Twitter user bemoaned the decision, explaining
how the lack of a decisive conclusion is more

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.230
harmful than good here.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:24.230
They said:

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.640
"As much as I liked it, I think 'Guardians
of the Galaxy.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.670
Vol 3' highlights a major problem with the
MCU at the moment.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:34.630
There's no REAL finality to anything, and
at that point, what's the point??!"

00:09:34.630 --> 00:09:35.950
It's a fair comment.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.930
With no endpoint in sight, all decisions are
simply about moving the franchise forward

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:43.200
— not necessarily toward anything impactful.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.010
On the other hand, a cinematic shared universe
is about the journey, not the destination,

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.690
so it's a different kind of storytelling model.

